WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
A practical dependency security roadmap begins with inventory and ownership.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:12.360
Know which libraries are in the product, why they are present, where they come from, which versions are shipped, and who is responsible for them.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.300
Remove what is unused.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.720
Keep SBOM-style records current enough to support updates, customer communication, and incident response.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.200
Select and acquire libraries deliberately.

6
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:33.100
Evaluate maintenance, documentation, security process, tests, portability, API design, and long-term support expectations.

7
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.600
Pin versions or otherwise control inputs.

8
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.500
Record provenance and integrity evidence.

9
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:47.880
Treat prebuilt binaries, remote fetching, and transitive dependencies as decisions that need visibility rather than conveniences hidden inside the build.

10
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.880
Integrate dependencies with clear boundaries.

11
00:00:50.980 --> 00:01:00.020
Review build options, optional components, compiler settings, ABI assumptions, runtime loading behavior, and platform differences.

12
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:07.800
Isolate risky APIs behind wrappers, validate inputs at library boundaries, normalize errors, and make resource ownership explicit.

13
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.620
Test the configuration the product actually ships.

14
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.980
Finally, govern the lifecycle.

15
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:22.440
Monitor vulnerabilities, assess real exposure, plan routine updates, prepare emergency update paths, and retire unsupported libraries.

16
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:29.500
Use tests, sanitizers, defensive fuzzing, runtime limits, and code review to reduce risk.

17
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:37.600
Keep decisions visible in issue trackers, release notes, and architecture records so future maintainers understand why the dependency exists.

18
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.300
Third-party library security is not a one-time install command.

19
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.800
It is an ongoing engineering practice tied to every release.
